<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-6">
	<h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-ink mb-1">Challenge your thinking — Delegation</h3>
	<p class="text-muted text-base mb-4">Start with C. That's the part you notice first.</p>
	<dl class="flex flex-col gap-4">
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">C · Consequences</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-base italic mb-1">
					What am I feeling? What am I about to do?
				</span>
				A flash of frustration, and the urge to step in and take the work
				back right now.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">A · Activating Event</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-base italic mb-1">
					What just happened?
				</span>
				I see a mistake happening on a task I've already delegated. My instinct
				is to take it back and do it myself.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">B · Beliefs</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink flex flex-col gap-2">
				<span class="block text-muted text-base italic">
					What do I prefer? What am I demanding?
				</span>
				<p>
					<span class="font-medium">Prefer — keep it:</span> I'd like this to go
					right the first time. Mistakes cost us time and reflect on my team.
				</p>
				<p>
					<span class="font-medium">Demand — dispute it:</span> They must take this
					as seriously as I do. If it goes wrong, it's awful, and it proves I'm a
					bad manager.
				</p>
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">D · Disputing</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<ul class="text-muted text-base italic mb-2 flex flex-col">
					<li>Is it awful, or just inconvenient?</li>
					<li>Can I actually stand it?</li>
					<li>Does one mistake make them — or me — a bad person?</li>
					<li>Who says it must not happen?</li>
				</ul>
				Nothing says they must care exactly as much as I do. A fixable mistake is
				inconvenient, not awful, and I can stand it. One miss doesn't make them a
				bad employee or me a bad leader.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">E · Effective Thinking</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-base italic mb-1">
					What's a truer way to see it?
				</span>
				I still want it done right. But mistakes in delegated work are normal and
				fixable, and my job is to develop people, not to guarantee perfect first
				drafts.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">F · Feeling &amp; Practice</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-base italic mb-1">
					What do I want to feel instead? What will I do right now?
				</span>
				Concern, not frustration. I take ten seconds to picture myself handling
				it calmly. Then I have a brief, specific conversation about what needs
				correcting and why, and I'm clear about the standard. I give them 24 hours
				to fix it — and I don't check in before then.
			</dd>
		</div>
	</dl>
	<p class="text-muted text-sm mt-5 pt-4 border-t border-rule">
		Ellis's model ends at E. F (Feeling &amp; Practice) extends it.
	</p>
</div>
